# Hostile DNS packets, one name each (main.jet HOSTILE), against the system
# resolver as Go's checkResponse and dnsmessage parser treat them.
# - A UDP packet that is not a reply to the question asked is ignored and the
#   wait goes on, so the honest reply after it wins: a wrong id, the QR bit
#   clear, under 12 bytes, another name, another type, or two questions.
#   The question's name is compared ignoring ASCII case.
# - With only forged packets, the lookup times out (Try again).
# - A malformed answer fails the name with Non-recoverable error (Go's
#   errCannotUnmarshalDNSMessage): an A record of the wrong size, record data
#   past the end, a compression pointer that does not point back, or a
#   reserved label type. The addresses before the fault stand.
# - Names are walked label by label, so a label byte of 0x80 or more is data,
#   and record types are 16 bits, so type 257 is not A.
# - A TCP answer to a truncated reply that does not match the question, or is
#   shorter than a header, fails the query (Go's errInvalidDNSResponse).
# Differences from musl: musl matches a UDP reply by id alone and keeps
# packets of 4 or more bytes. It takes any TCP answer, and gives up the whole
# exchange when one is under 13 bytes. It skips names by scanning for a byte
# outside 1..127, and it reads only a record type's low byte.
requires: os-name:linux
requires: tool:sh+unshare+ip+mount
requires: probe unshare --user --map-root-user --net --mount true # unprivileged user namespaces are enabled
fixture: system_resolver_follows_go_against_a_local_dns_server.fixture
env: NO_COLOR=1
env: JET_STORE_DIR={work}/cache
step build: jet build main.jet
exit: 0
step resolve: unshare --user --map-root-user --net --mount sh inside.sh hostile
exit: 0
stdout: = system_resolver_ignores_forged_dns_replies_and_fails_malformed_ones.out
